  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B013",
        "laureates": [
          "Paul B. Corkum",
          "Maciej Lewenstein"
        ],
        "citation": "For the recollision picture and its quantitative quantum theory of strong-field interactions."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B044",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The recollision picture revealed how an electron driven by an intense laser can return to its parent ion, turning strong-field interactions into a way to probe atomic dynamics. Corkum's physical picture and Lewenstein's quantitative quantum model are complementary contributions to a framework tested across many systems and central to later attosecond science. This is a fundamental advance in understanding electron motion, not simply a useful technique.",
    "case_against": "The discovery boundary needs careful handling: high-harmonic generation and attosecond measurement were developed by many groups, and the broad label could blur the foundational recollision theory with later experimental advances. The citation should stay focused on the physical picture and its quantitative model.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson supports the recollision picture and Lewenstein's quantum formulation, while cautioning that the prize should distinguish this foundation from later attosecond pulse generation and measurement.",
        "response": "I agree with that boundary. The citation I propose names the recollision picture and its quantitative theory; it does not claim the later development of every pulse-generation or measurement method. That narrower scope makes the contribution clearer while recognizing why those later methods became possible."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson notes that quantum error correction has a firmly established theory and increasingly capable demonstrations, while large-scale fault tolerance remains unrealized and the early contributions followed distinct paths.",
        "response": "That is a useful distinction between a foundational result and a still-unfinished technological goal. The same distinction supports judging B013 on its established physics: recollision theory has been tested across systems and has supported sustained experimental work, without requiring that every later application be complete. The attribution here is also more compact, though the broader experimental history still deserves care."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise boundary between the foundational recollision framework and later high-harmonic and attosecond experimental advances needs historical review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile notes a close collaboration with Anne L'Huillier and coauthored work with the Lund attosecond group. Neither is among the proposed laureates, but the connection to adjacent attosecond work should be disclosed for recusal consideration."
  }
}
